  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 254 AM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\n(Friday Night - Saturday)\n\nThe vast majority of the region should stay dry Friday night through \nthe day on Saturday, though a few spot showers are possible mainly \nacross northeast Missouri and west-central Illinois. This is where \nthe residual surface boundary may be located with a weak midlevel \nshortwave trough also moving across Missouri early Saturday morning. \nA mostly cloudy sky is favored otherwise, with very mild lows (mid \n50s to near 60 degrees) and highs near 70 degrees.\n\n\n(Saturday Night - Sunday)\n\nDeterministic guidance shows better agreement with the timing/track \nof another midlevel shortwave trough moving out of the south-central \nPlains across southeast Missouri and southwest Illinois late \nSaturday night into Sunday morning. At least scattered light rain \nshowers are expected ahead of this feature. The key word is \"light\" \nthough. While LREF 24-hour probabilities for measurable rainfall are \npretty high (60-75%) in southeast Missouri and southwest Illinois, \nchances for at least 0.25\" drop to 25-35%. \n\n\n(Sunday Night - Wednesday)\n\nEnsemble guidance remains in good agreement that a deep mid/upper \nlevel trough will move out of the intermountain west Sunday night \ninto Monday. Cluster analyses of the 500-hPa pattern generally show \na closed, possibly cutoff, mid/upper level low across the central \nPlains on Tuesday, with deep south/southwest flow aloft across the \nmid-Mississippi Valley. Ahead of the closed mid/upper level low, a \nsurface low is expected to slowly move into the Upper Midwest, with \na cold front draped to its south. This boundary is expected to \ngradually move eastward in time, but exactly when is not certain. \nRegardless, the pattern still appears favorable for a long duration, \nsignificant (>1\") rain event across the CWA through the middle of \nnext week. LREF probabilities for at least 1\" through Wednesday \nafternoon are 60-90% from southeast to northwest, and 20-50% for \n2+\". While this event is largely 4-6+ days out and things can \ncertainly change, there does appear to be increasing potential for \nsubstantial drought improvements across the region.",
